%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:H_6985E217] ## ![](text:image:1c52)
%[text]{"align":"center"} Adelie penguins plodding through [snow](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2007_Snow-Hill-Island_Luyten-De-Hauwere-Adelie-Penguin-20.jpg). 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_04BAFB5C] ## Load and set up necessary data: 
filename = "penguins.csv";
penguins = readtable(filename);
penguins.species = categorical(penguins.species);
penguins.island = categorical(penguins.island);
penguins.sex = categorical(penguins.sex);
penguins1 = rmmissing(penguins);
chinstrap = penguins1(penguins1.species == "Chinstrap",:);
gentoo = penguins1(penguins1.species == "Gentoo",:); 
adelie = penguins1(penguins1.species == "Adelie",:);
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_829F593F] ## Problem 1:
%[text] Use  `scatter() to p`lot bill depth versus bill length (hint: `penguins1.bill_length_mm`) for all penguins. Don't forget to include labels! 





%[text] ![](text:image:8b88) **Bonus:** As we did in the [Biosciences: Working with Data](https://github.com/MathWorks-Teaching-Resources/Biosciences-Working-With-Data) module, use `gscatter()` to break this plot up by species. 





%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8BA1ED8E] ## Problem 2: 
%[text] Create a box plot of bill length for all species of penguins (label the x and y axes!)




%[text] Create a box plot of bill depth for all species of penguins. 




